9. What would we do if there was no wifi at that moment? 
-Off-Grid / Outdoor Scenarios (No Wi-Fi)
Wi-Fi renders the wearable useless outdoors. Two field-ready alternatives:   
-GSM / Cellular Module: Interface an ultra-compact LTE-M / NB-IoT or 2G/4G module (e.g., SIM7080G or SIM800L) to send direct SMS alerts or HTTP webhooks anywhere there is cellular coverage.
-BLE Smartphone Gateway: Configure the ESP32-C3 as a Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) peripheral. It advertises to an app on the wearer's smartphone, which handles cellular routing and GPS querying.

10. What if the person is on a wheel chair?
=> Standard fall algorithms fail for wheelchair users because staying stationary is their baseline state, not an emergency indicator.
-Chair-Tip Detection: Detect structural overturns by monitoring sudden, persistent roll/pitch tilt $> 60^\circ$.
-Slide-Out Detection: Combine a chest-mounted IMU with an auxiliary seat-occupancy sensor (e.g., a thin Force-Sensitive Resistor mat on the wheelchair cushion). If the seat switch disconnects while the chest sensor registers high acceleration, an alert is triggered. 

11. How long must the buzzer be on for? 
=> Cancel Window (First 20–30s): Fast, intermittent pulsing (e.g., 200 ms ON, 200 ms OFF) to alert the user that a countdown has started.
- Post-Alert Search Beacon: If the alert dispatches (user is incapacitated), shift to a single chirp every 3–5 seconds. This conserves battery life while providing an acoustic beacon for first responders without causing auditory trauma to the victim.

12. What is the person falls near the hip and before it can send a signal, the machine is only destroyed, what then?
=> Physical & Mechanical Hardening
You can significantly lower the risk of hardware destruction through basic mechanical enclosure design:
- Potting / Resin Encapsulation: Fill the 3D-printed enclosure around the ESP32 and MPU-6050 with silicone sealant, hot glue, or electronics potting resin. This prevents solder joint fractures, wire tears, and component shearing when subjected to high G-forces.
- TPU Flexible Bumper: Print an outer protective boot out of flexible TPU (Thermoplastic Polyurethane) or add a silicone sleeve to absorb structural shock.
- Ergonomic Mounting: Place the unit on the upper sternum or side waist (hip bone recess). Humans naturally fall forward onto knees/hands or backward onto buttocks—direct impacts onto the side hip or upper chest are statistically rare.

